Gitr licensing
==============

Effective 2026-11-10, the repository owner offers the copyright-controlled
Gitr contributions identified in Documentation/gitr/license-scope.json under
either of the following licenses, at the recipient's option:

  * MIT, in LICENSE-MIT
  * Apache License, Version 2.0, in LICENSE-APACHE

SPDX expression for that contribution grant: MIT OR Apache-2.0.

This is an additional license grant for the owner's contributions. It does
not withdraw licenses already granted. The source-origin record identifies
the reviewed files and historical paths. It does not claim ownership of
third-party material within a file or grant rights the owner does not hold.
Any such material remains subject to its original license and notices.

Third-party and inherited material
----------------------------------

Third-party code, modifications of third-party code, imported documentation,
fixtures, notices and archived material retain their applicable upstream
terms. In particular:

  * Inherited Git material retains COPYING and any file-specific notices.
    COPYING expressly selects GPL version 2 unless otherwise stated.
  * vendor/grit/grit-git retains GPL-2.0-only, including its local adaptations.
  * vendor/grit/grit-lib and vendor/grit/grit-test-support retain MIT.
  * vendor/rbit-workflow retains MIT OR Apache-2.0 and both license texts.
  * Archived material and external dependencies retain their own grants.

No root license, source-origin classification or package metadata overrides
those terms. The project contribution grant excludes vendor/, inherited Git
material, and Documentation/gitr/archive/ unless an explicit record states
otherwise. Future imports must retain their actual licenses and provenance.

Current combined executable
---------------------------

The current root package unconditionally links the GPL-2.0-only historical
grit-git CLI. Distribution of that combined executable must comply with
GPL-2.0-only and all applicable third-party notices and obligations. Use the
MIT option for the dual-licensed project contributions in that composition.
The --isolated-git runtime option does not remove grit-git from the build.

This repository is therefore not a wholly MIT- or Apache-licensed product.
Changing Gitr's own grant does not make Apache-2.0-only or GPLv3-only code
compatible with the current GPL-2.0-only executable. A different combined
license requires a compatible source/dependency composition or sufficient
additional permission from the relevant rights holders.

See Documentation/gitr/licensing.md for the source map, import rules, and
the path to compatible future compositions. Keep LICENSE, LICENSE-MIT,
LICENSE-APACHE, COPYING and applicable upstream notices with distributions.
